    ]
  },
  "descriptionKey": "- Chick culling is the killing of newly hatched male chicks, which cannot lay eggs.\n- Hens that lay eggs come from different breeds than chickens raised for meat (called \"broilers\"). The male chicks of egg-laying breeds grow more slowly and give less meat than broilers, so raising them is not profitable.\n- Billions of male baby chicks are estimated to be killed around the world every year.\n- This data shows the legal status of chick culling, not how widely it is practiced.\n- Bans in Austria and France still allow the killing of baby chicks that are used as animal feed.\n- Bans prohibit killing chicks within the country, but not exporting them. After Germany's ban, male chicks have reportedly been transported to other countries where culling remains legal.\n- There is no EU-wide ban on chick culling. The European Commission has committed to proposing EU-wide rules to end the systematic killing of male chicks by the end of 2026.\n- The main alternative to chick culling is \"in-ovo sexing\", a technology that determines the sex of the embryo inside the egg, so that eggs with male embryos can be discarded well before hatching.\n- Ducklings are also killed soon after hatching in major duck-egg producing countries like China. No law specifically bans the culling of ducklings.\n- France's ban on chick culling explicitly excludes ducklings. In foie gras production it is the female ducklings that are killed, as their livers are less profitable.",
